% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
% Abstract (Strictly <= 200 words)
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{abstract}
Diagnostic latency in Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) often exceeds three years, motivating substantial research into automated machine learning triage using brief questionnaires and resting-state fMRI. However, published literature exhibits pervasive methodological vulnerabilities. This investigation presents an adversarial methodological audit and reproducible benchmark across four public questionnaire cohorts (Adult, Adolescent, Child, Toddler; $N = 1,876$) and a multi-site resting-state fMRI cohort from ABIDE-I ($N = 120$ subjects across four imaging centers). First, we establish that widely reported 98--100\,\% questionnaire accuracies represent an arithmetic tautology: target labels in public UCI datasets are deterministic functions of item sums ($\sum A_i \ge \tau$, 100.0\,\% concordance), rendering machine learning non-incremental over standard scoring rules. Second, under Leave-One-Site-Out (LOSO) cross-validation with fold-internal ComBat harmonization, functional connectivity models achieve 55.0\,\% accuracy (95\,\% CI: [46.7\,\%, 64.2\,\%]). Exact binomial tests ($p = 0.315$) and 1,000 within-site label permutations ($p = 0.215$) indicate performance is indistinguishable from chance under statistical power constraints. Third, we formulate an engineering feasibility prototype for client-side decision support: split conformal prediction bounds diagnostic uncertainty (95.5\,\% coverage) by isolating borderline presentations, while client-side edge compilation enables zero-upload inference in 0.45~ms. We release all deduplicated datasets, scripts, and audited baselines.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Autism Spectrum Disorder, Methodological Audit, Label Leakage, ABIDE-I, Leave-One-Site-Out Cross-Validation, ComBat Harmonization, Conformal Prediction, Model Calibration, Edge Computing.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
% Section I: Introduction
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}

Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition characterized by persistent atypicalities in reciprocal social interaction, sensory processing, and restricted, repetitive patterns of behavior. Recent epidemiological monitoring by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) Autism and Developmental Disabilities Monitoring (ADDM) Network estimates ASD prevalence between 1 in 36 (2.7\,\%) and 1 in 31 (3.2\,\%) children in the United States \cite{maenner2023}. While early evidence-based behavioral therapies substantially improve longitudinal functional independence, clinical diagnostic latency from initial caregiver concern to formal specialist diagnosis regularly spans 36 to 60 months. This diagnostic bottleneck stems from severe shortages of licensed specialists certified to administer gold-standard observational evaluations, including the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule, Second Edition (ADOS-2) \cite{lord2012ados}.

To address specialist scarcity, extensive machine learning research has evaluated automated screening algorithms. Two primary computational paradigms dominate the literature: (i) supervised tabular classifiers applied to brief behavioral screening questionnaires (such as the 10-item Autism Spectrum Quotient, AQ-10 \cite{allison2012}, and Quantitative Checklist for Autism in Toddlers, Q-CHAT-10), and (ii) deep neural architectures trained on resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging (rs-fMRI) connectomes from multi-center repositories such as the Autism Brain Imaging Data Exchange (ABIDE-I) \cite{di2014}. Tabular questionnaire studies regularly report held-out test accuracies between 98.0\,\% and 100.0\,\% \cite{thabtah2019, raj2020}, while neuroimaging studies report cross-sectional diagnostic accuracies exceeding 70.0\,\% to 75.0\,\% using autoencoders, graph convolutional networks, and transformers \cite{heinsfeld2018, parisot2018, kan2022bnt}.

However, clinical translation of these computational models remains nonexistent. In this work, we demonstrate that published performance metrics are driven by pervasive methodological vulnerabilities:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Circular Label Construction:} In public behavioral questionnaire benchmarks, diagnostic target classes are generated directly from deterministic cutoffs on item response sums. Consequently, trained classifiers fit an artificial step function rather than latent clinical phenotype signals.
    \item \textbf{Validation Leakage and Scanner Overfitting:} Multi-site rs-fMRI benchmarks commonly utilize random in-sample cross-validation splits. Because acquisition hardware signatures strongly separate scanning centers, models memorize site-specific artifact baselines rather than invariant neuropathological signatures.
    \item \textbf{Uncalibrated Point Estimates in Deployment:} Deployed screening applications force binary classifications without coverage guarantees, producing uncalibrated predictions precisely on clinically ambiguous presentations.
\end{enumerate}

To address these vulnerabilities, we execute an empirical audit and formulate an engineering feasibility prototype for client-side decision support. We summarize our contributions as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Formal Behavioral Tautology Audit:} Across four public developmental cohorts ($N = 1,876$ total instances), we prove that public UCI benchmark labels match deterministic integer summation rules with 100.0\,\% concordance. Through repeated $5 \times 10$ cross-validation, we demonstrate that machine learning models provide zero incremental value over standard arithmetic scoring keys.
    \item \textbf{Controlled Multi-Site Neuroimaging Benchmark:} We evaluate functional connectomes across four ABIDE-I acquisition centers ($N = 120$) under strict Leave-One-Site-Out (LOSO) cross-validation with fold-internal ComBat harmonization. Statistical power analysis (minimum detectable accuracy 62.7\,\% at 80\,\% power), exact binomial tests, and 1,000 within-site label permutations indicate performance is indistinguishable from chance under statistical power constraints (55.0\,\% accuracy, $p = 0.215$).
    \item \textbf{Client-Side Decision Support Prototype:} We formulate an inductive split conformal prediction layer that bounds marginal test coverage (95.5\,\%) while systematically flagging borderline cases ($\sum A_i \in \{6, 7\}$) as uncertain. We compile the model into an ultra-compact 2.4~kB ONNX graph executing in 0.45~ms client-side with zero data egress under strict Content Security Policies.
\end{itemize}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
% Section II: Related Work
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Behavioral Questionnaire Screening}
The AQ-10 was developed by Allison et al. \cite{allison2012} as a clinical triage instrument derived through psychometric item reduction from the full 50-item Autism Spectrum Quotient. In clinical validation, an integer threshold of $\sum A_i \ge 6$ yielded 0.88 sensitivity and 0.91 specificity. Subsequent computational works published on the UCI Machine Learning Repository \cite{uci_adult, uci_adolescent, uci_child} applied machine learning classifiers, reporting near-perfect diagnostic classification \cite{thabtah2019, raj2020}. However, these evaluations failed to analyze the target column generation mechanism or address identical duplicate rows.

\subsection{Resting-State fMRI Connectomics on ABIDE-I}
The ABIDE-I consortium \cite{di2014} aggregated resting-state fMRI scans across 17 independent international acquisition sites. Early deep learning investigations pooled subjects across sites under standard random 10-fold cross-validation, reporting 70.0\,\% accuracy with stacked autoencoders \cite{heinsfeld2018} and 69.5\,\% with graph convolutional networks \cite{parisot2018}. However, Abraham et al. \cite{abraham2017} demonstrated that random splits cause substantial validation leakage because scanner-specific acquisition parameters (repetition time, head coils, gradient fields) confound in-sample partitions. Under Leave-Center-Out validation, linear estimators achieved 64.0\,\%--67.0\,\%, demonstrating that inter-site variance severely degrades out-of-scanner generalization. Modern graph transformer models such as Brain Network Transformer \cite{kan2022bnt} achieved 73.2\,\% on in-sample splits, but remain susceptible to domain shifts under strict site holdouts.

