\subsection{Questionnaire Benchmark Datasets and Deduplication}
We evaluated four public developmental screening cohorts ($N = 1,876$ total unique subjects):
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Adult ASD Cohort ($N = 667$):} Hosted on the UCI repository \cite{uci_adult} with 704 raw entries. Superficial full-row deduplication across all 21 raw columns yields 699 distinct rows (removing 5 exact duplicates). However, clinical deduplication across the 10 questionnaire items and core demographic identifiers ($A_1$--$A_{10}$, age, sex, diagnosis) identifies 37 duplicate patient submissions, retaining $N = 667$ unique clinical subjects.
    \item \textbf{Adolescent ASD Cohort ($N = 97$):} Covering ages 12--16 on UCI \cite{uci_adolescent} with 104 raw entries (103 distinct full rows). Feature-level deduplication removes 7 duplicate patient records, retaining $N = 97$ unique subjects.
    \item \textbf{Child ASD Cohort ($N = 262$):} Covering ages 4--11 on UCI \cite{uci_child} with 292 raw entries (290 distinct full rows). Feature-level deduplication removes 30 duplicate records, retaining $N = 262$ unique subjects.
    \item \textbf{Toddler Q-CHAT-10 Cohort ($N = 850$):} Hosted on Kaggle \cite{thabtah2018toddler} with 1,054 raw entries evaluating toddlers aged 12--36 months. Deduplication across item responses and demographics removes 204 duplicate submissions, retaining $N = 850$ unique subjects.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Behavioral Tautology and Decision Rule Formulation}
In the AQ-10 instrument, each response is scored as a binary value $A_i \in \{0, 1\}$. We formulated the deterministic Benchmark Scoring Rule baseline:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{y}_{\text{rule}} = \mathbb{I}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{10} A_i \ge \tau\right)
    \label{eq:rule_cutoff}
\end{equation}
where $\tau \in \mathbb{N}$ denotes the screening threshold. We emphasize that $\tau = 7$ is the dataset's label-defining threshold for AQ-10 (and $\tau = 4$ for Q-CHAT-10), which is distinct from the clinical referral cutoff of $\tau = 6$ psychometrically validated by Allison et al. \cite{allison2012}. Our audit explicitly evaluates this dataset label-defining cutoff ($\tau = 7$) to test whether published ML claims extract genuine diagnostic phenotypes or simply fit the repository's programmatic summation rule.

To test whether statistical learners extract nonlinear interactions beyond this summation, we trained four base classifiers: Logistic Regression (L2 regularized), LightGBM \cite{ke2017lightgbm}, XGBoost \cite{chen2016xgboost}, and CatBoost \cite{prokhorenkova2018catboost}. A logistic meta-classifier aggregates out-of-fold base probability estimates $\hat{p}_k(\mathbf{x})$:
\begin{equation}
    P(y = 1 \mid \mathbf{x}) = \sigma\left(w_0 + \sum_{k=1}^K w_k \hat{p}_k(\mathbf{x})\right)
    \label{eq:stacking_meta}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(z) = (1 + e^{-z})^{-1}$ and $K = 4$.

\subsection{Controlled Multi-Site Neuroimaging Benchmark}
Resting-state fMRI scans were obtained from the ABIDE-I repository \cite{di2014}, preprocessed through the Configurable Pipeline for the Analysis of Connectomes (CPAC) under the Preprocessed Connectomes Project \cite{craddock2013pcp}. To establish a controlled multi-site pilot benchmark, we curated 120 subjects across four clinical imaging centers: Oregon Health \& Science University (OHSU, $N=25$), Olin Neuropsychiatry Research Center (OLIN, $N=28$), University of Pittsburgh (PITT, $N=50$), and San Diego State University (SDSU, $N=17$). Scans were parcellated using the Craddock 200 (CC200) functional clustering atlas ($P = 200$ regions of interest) \cite{craddock2012}.

Pairwise Pearson correlation matrices $\mathbf{R} \in \mathbb{R}^{200 \times 200}$ were transformed via Fisher's $z$-transformation:
\begin{equation}
    z_{uv} = \frac{1}{2} \ln\left(\frac{1 + r_{uv}}{1 - r_{uv}}\right)
    \label{eq:fisher_z}
\end{equation}
Extracting the upper triangle yields an undirected connectome vector $\mathbf{x}_i \in \mathbb{R}^M$ where $M = \frac{P(P - 1)}{2} = 19,900$ unique functional connectivity edges per subject.

Under Leave-One-Site-Out cross-validation across $S = 4$ sites, for each held-out site $s \in \{1, \dots, S\}$, models are trained strictly on data from the remaining $S - 1$ centers:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}^{(s)} = \bigcup_{j \neq s} \mathcal{D}_j, \quad \mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{(s)} = \mathcal{D}_s
    \label{eq:loso_split}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Fold-Internal ComBat Empirical Bayes Harmonization}
To mitigate scanner batch effects without test leakage, we implemented parametric Empirical Bayes ComBat \cite{johnson2007combat} strictly inside training folds. Let $y_{ijg}$ represent connectivity edge $g$ for subject $j$ at site $i$:
\begin{equation}
    y_{ijg} = \alpha_g + \mathbf{X}_{ij}\boldsymbol{\beta}_g + \gamma_{ig} + \delta_{ig}\epsilon_{ijg}
    \label{eq:combat_formulation}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_g$ represents the overall reference mean of edge $g$, $\mathbf{X}_{ij}$ denotes biological covariates with coefficients $\boldsymbol{\beta}_g$, $\gamma_{ig}$ is the site-specific additive shift, $\delta_{ig}$ is the multiplicative scaling factor, and $\epsilon_{ijg} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_g^2)$. 

Parameters $\hat{\alpha}_g$, $\hat{\gamma}_{ig}$, and $\hat{\delta}_{ig}$ are estimated exclusively on $\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}^{(s)}$. For unseen test subjects in $\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{(s)}$, applying the training reference parameters ($\hat{\alpha}_g, \hat{\delta}_{g, \text{ref}}$) represents a zero-calibration standardization protocol:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{x}_{\text{test}, g}^* = \frac{\mathbf{x}_{\text{test}, g} - \hat{\alpha}_g}{\hat{\delta}_{g, \text{ref}}} + \hat{\alpha}_g
    \label{eq:combat_unseen}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\delta}_{g, \text{ref}}$ represents the pooled training dispersion. We emphasize that site-specific batch shifts ($\gamma_{\text{test}, g}, \delta_{\text{test}, g}$) cannot be estimated for previously unobserved scanners without test calibration data. This zero-calibration standardization protocol projects unseen test scans onto the training reference distribution label-free, preventing test data leakage while standardizing baseline dispersion. Following harmonization, univariate ANOVA $F$-test feature selection extracts the top $k = 250$ edges exclusively on training subjects, avoiding feature-selection leakage.

\subsection{Split Conformal Prediction Framework}
To replace uncalibrated point estimates with distribution-free coverage guarantees, we implemented inductive split conformal prediction \cite{angelopoulos2023, vovk2005}. The training data is split into proper training ($n_{\text{train}} = 373$) and calibration sets ($n_{\text{cal}} = 160$). For binary classification $y \in \{0, 1\}$, nonconformity scores are computed on calibration samples:
\begin{equation}
    s_i = 1 - \hat{p}(y_i \mid \mathbf{x}_i)
    \label{eq:nonconformity}
\end{equation}
Given significance level $\alpha \in (0, 1)$, acceptance threshold $\hat{q}$ is the empirical quantile:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{q} = \text{Quantile}\left(\{s_i\}_{i=1}^{n_{\text{cal}}}; \frac{\lceil(n_{\text{cal}} + 1)(1 - \alpha)\rceil}{n_{\text{cal}}}\right)
    \label{eq:qhat}
\end{equation}
At test time, the conformal prediction set $C(\mathbf{x})$ is constructed without access to true labels:
\begin{equation}
    C(\mathbf{x}) = \left\{ y \in \{0, 1\} : 1 - \hat{p}(y \mid \mathbf{x}) \le \hat{q} \right\}
    \label{eq:pred_set}
\end{equation}
When $\hat{q} < 0.5$, singleton sets dominate. Any instance where the set is empty ($|C(\mathbf{x})| = 0$) or ambiguous ($|C(\mathbf{x})| = 2$) violates single-class certainty and is flagged as \emph{Clinically Borderline / Uncertain}.

\subsection{Knowledge Distillation and Browser Edge Compilation}
To evaluate client-side edge feasibility, we compressed the linear connectome estimator into an ultra-compact student model via knowledge distillation \cite{hinton2015}. The student model optimizes a convex combination of cross-entropy and temperature-scaled Kullback-Leibler divergence against teacher logit $z_t$:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{distill}} = (1 - \lambda)\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}(y, \sigma(z_s)) + \lambda T^2 \mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}\left(\sigma\left(\frac{z_s}{T}\right), \sigma\left(\frac{z_t}{T}\right)\right)
    \label{eq:distill_loss}
\end{equation}
with temperature $T = 2.0$ and weighting parameter $\lambda = 0.5$. The distilled student retains only 50 functional edges and is compiled into an Open Neural Network Exchange (ONNX) graph \cite{onnxruntime2023}. Client-side execution in browser memory is isolated under strict Content Security Policies (`connect-src 'none'`) preventing remote network egress.

