\textbf{Subgroup Fairness Audit:} Disaggregating the held-out adult test set by sex yields 100.0\,\% accuracy for males ($N = 75$, 95\,\% Clopper-Pearson CI: [95.2\,\%, 100.0\,\%]) and 100.0\,\% for females ($N = 59$, 95\,\% CI: [93.9\,\%, 100.0\,\%]). While equitable performance is clinically vital given diagnostic sex disparities \cite{lai2014}, equal performance here is an inevitable mathematical consequence of target column derivation from the scoring sum, and must not be misinterpreted as evidence of algorithmic demographic fairness.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\safeinclude[width=0.92\linewidth]{figures/fig_rule_vs_ml_ablation.pdf}
\caption{Empirical comparison of the Benchmark Scoring Rule baseline against Logistic Regression and Stacking Ensemble across repeated cross-validation ($5 \times 10$ CV). The simple scoring rule matches or outperforms complex ML across all cohorts.}
\label{fig:rule_vs_ml}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Conformal Uncertainty Quantification}
On the adult test set ($n_{\text{test}} = 134$) with calibration set $n_{\text{cal}} = 160$, split conformal prediction at $\alpha = 0.10$ produced quantile threshold $\hat{q} = 0.2574$. Marginal empirical test coverage reached 95.52\,\% ($128/134$, 95\,\% Wilson score CI: [90.58\,\%, 97.93\,\%]), satisfying the nominal 90.0\,\% guarantee. Class-conditional coverage was 98.94\,\% ($93/94$ controls) and 87.50\,\% ($35/40$ ASD cases), where all 6 uncovered instances corresponded to empty prediction sets.

Prediction set sizes comprised 95.52\,\% singleton sets ($128/134$, $|C(\mathbf{x})| = 1$), 0.0\,\% pair sets ($0/134$, $|C(\mathbf{x})| = 2$), and 4.48\,\% empty sets ($6/134$, $|C(\mathbf{x})| = 0$). Exactly 6 cases (4.48\,\%) were flagged as clinically uncertain ($|C(\mathbf{x})| \neq 1$). Inspection confirmed that all six flagged instances had integer sums $\sum A_i \in \{6, 7\}$, coinciding with the borderline threshold of the screening instrument.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\safeinclude[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_conformal_calibration.pdf}
\caption{Split conformal prediction evaluation on the held-out adult cohort. (Left) Empirical test coverage meets nominal $1 - \alpha$ bounds. (Right) Rate of clinically uncertain cases ($|C(\mathbf{x})| \neq 1$) systematically isolates borderline cases.}
\label{fig:conformal_cal}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Multi-Site rs-fMRI LOSO Evaluation and Site Heterogeneity}
Table~\ref{tab:fmri_loso} summarizes per-site and aggregate performance on ABIDE-I under Leave-One-Site-Out cross-validation. Aggregate LOSO accuracy was 55.00\,\% ($66/120$ correct), with Balanced Accuracy of 54.67\,\%, ROC-AUC of 0.6098, and F1 of 0.4906. The 95\,\% bootstrap confidence interval was $[46.67\,\%, 64.17\,\%]$, which encompasses the 50.0\,\% chance baseline.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Per-Site Held-Out fMRI LOSO Results on ABIDE-I ($N=120$)}
\label{tab:fmri_loso}
\centering
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Site} & \textbf{$N$} & \textbf{Acc. (95\,\% Wilson CI)} & \textbf{Two-sided $p$} & \textbf{Holm $p_{\text{adj}}$} & \textbf{ROC-AUC} \\
\midrule
OHSU & 25 & 48.0\,\% [30.0\,\%, 66.5\,\%] & $p = 1.000$ & $p = 1.000$ & 0.5449 \\
OLIN & 28 & 60.7\,\% [42.4\,\%, 76.4\,\%] & $p = 0.345$ & $p = 1.000$ & 0.5663 \\
PITT & 50 & 46.0\,\% [33.0\,\%, 59.6\,\%] & $p = 0.672$ & $p = 1.000$ & 0.6026 \\
