\subsection{Hypothesis Testing, Permutation Protocols, and Statistical Power}
To test whether the observed 55.0\,\% LOSO accuracy reflects genuine discriminative signal:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Statistical Power Analysis:} For a two-sided one-sample proportion test against chance ($p_0 = 0.50$) at $\alpha = 0.05$ with $N = 120$, the minimum detectable effect size at 80\,\% statistical power is \textbf{62.7\,\%} accuracy. The observed accuracy of 55.0\,\% falls substantially below this statistical sensitivity threshold.
    \item \textbf{Exact Binomial Test:} Evaluating $k = 66$ successes out of $N = 120$ trials under null hypothesis $p_0 = 0.50$ yields two-sided $p = 0.3153$ (one-sided $p = 0.1577$).
    \item \textbf{Within-Site Label Permutation Test ($B = 1,000$):} Permuting diagnostic labels exclusively within each acquisition center preserves center-specific class prevalences while refitting the full pipeline (ComBat harmonization, ANOVA selection, L2 classifier). The resulting empirical null distribution exhibited mean accuracy $50.18\,\% \pm 5.51\,\%$, yielding empirical $p = \frac{1 + 214}{1001} = \mathbf{0.2148}$.
    \item \textbf{Global Label Permutation Baseline ($B = 1,000$):} Permuting labels across the entire cohort yielded empirical null mean $48.87\,\% \pm 5.45\,\%$, with empirical $p = \frac{1 + 142}{1001} = \mathbf{0.1429}$.
\end{itemize}

Because both permutation tests and exact binomial tests exceed $\alpha = 0.05$, no evidence of above-chance generalization was observed. Deep architectures evaluated under identical LOSO splits overfitted severely: Brain Network Transformer \cite{kan2022bnt} achieved 45.83\,\% accuracy.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\safeinclude[width=0.92\linewidth]{figures/fig_loso_permutation.pdf}
\caption{Empirical null distribution from 1,000 within-site label permutations versus observed LOSO accuracy (55.0\,\%, $p = 0.215$). The null distribution confirms that performance is indistinguishable from chance under statistical power constraints.}
\label{fig:loso_perm}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Scanner Fingerprint and Confound Audits}
To quantify residual hardware signatures, we trained a multi-class logistic classifier to predict acquisition site from connectome edges under 5-fold cross-validation. The majority-class baseline (PITT, $N=50$) is $50 / 120 = \mathbf{41.67\,\%}$, while balanced-accuracy chance is $1/4 = \mathbf{25.00\,\%}$. On unharmonized connectomes, site prediction achieved 73.33\,\% $\pm$ 5.00\,\% accuracy (balanced accuracy 67.17\,\%), confirming strong scanner fingerprints. Fold-internal ComBat standardization successfully attenuated these hardware artifacts.

Correlating out-of-fold predicted probabilities against subject phenotypic characteristics revealed:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Participant Age: $r = -0.1839$, raw $p = 0.0444$, Holm-adjusted $p = \mathbf{0.1332}$.
    \item Head Motion (mean Framewise Displacement): $r = -0.1399$, raw $p = 0.1276$, Holm-adjusted $p = \mathbf{0.2552}$.
    \item Biological Sex: point-biserial $r = 0.0196$, raw $p = 0.8315$, Holm-adjusted $p = \mathbf{0.8315}$.
\end{itemize}
When regressing out age, sex, and head motion from connectivity features inside training folds via ordinary least squares, out-of-site LOSO accuracy was 58.33\,\%, which remains statistically indistinguishable from baseline ($p > 0.05$).

\subsection{Knowledge Distillation and Browser Inference Efficiency}
The 50-edge linear student model distilled from the teacher connectome representation achieved 59.17\,\% LOSO accuracy ($71/120$ correct). To evaluate whether the student differed significantly from the teacher (55.00\,\%, $66/120$), we conducted a subject-level paired McNemar test. The $2 \times 2$ contingency table comprised: both correct ($n_{11} = 61$), teacher correct / student wrong ($n_{10} = 5$), teacher wrong / student correct ($n_{01} = 10$), and both wrong ($n_{00} = 44$). The Edwards continuity-corrected McNemar test statistic was $\chi^2 = 1.0667$ with exact two-sided binomial $p = \mathbf{0.3018}$. This establishes that the minor 5-subject shift is statistically marginal, confirming representation preservation.

The distilled model compiles into a 2.4~kB ONNX graph. In browser memory under ONNX Runtime Web \cite{onnxruntime2023} (WebAssembly backend), single-instance inference latency averaged 0.45~ms (IQR: [0.41, 0.52]~ms across 1,000 runs following 100 warm-up runs). Sandboxed client-side execution ensures complete patient data isolation with zero remote transmission.

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
% Section V: Threats to Validity & Limitations
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Threats to Validity and Limitations}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Controlled Pilot Benchmark Sample Size:} While 120 subjects across four acquisition centers provide rigorous controlled validation, larger cohorts ($N > 1,000$) are necessary to detect subtle effect sizes below our 62.7\,\% power threshold.
    \item \textbf{Questionnaire Instrument Constraints:} The AQ-10 is an abbreviated community screening instrument, not a diagnostic confirmation. Because public benchmark labels are generated from item sums, they cannot validate real-world clinical discrimination between neurodevelopmental conditions.
    \item \textbf{Preprocessing Latency Disparity:} Although client-side inference of the 50-edge linear graph requires 0.45~ms, spatial normalization and 4D BOLD motion correction currently require offline server pipelines.
\end{enumerate}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
% Section VI: Ethical Considerations & Data Availability
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Ethical Considerations and Data Availability}

Automated neurodevelopmental screening tools must never be deployed as autonomous diagnostic arbiters. Algorithmic outputs should serve strictly as triage decision-support layers to prioritize clinical evaluations. All public datasets analyzed in this work are fully de-identified and open-access under Creative Commons licenses. Full reproduction scripts, deduplicated datasets, and metrics are available at: \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/asd-screening-audit}.

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
% Section VII: Conclusion
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Conclusion}

We presented an adversarial methodological audit and reproducible benchmark for machine learning in Autism Spectrum Disorder screening. We demonstrated that widely cited 98--100\,\% questionnaire accuracies represent an arithmetic tautology wherein machine learning provides zero incremental value over simple integer addition. Furthermore, under Leave-One-Site-Out cross-validation on ABIDE-I, functional connectivity estimators achieved 55.0\,\% accuracy, which exact binomial tests ($p = 0.315$) and 1,000 within-site label permutations ($p = 0.215$) indicate performance is indistinguishable from chance under statistical power constraints. Finally, we demonstrated that split conformal prediction and client-side ONNX compilation establish an engineering feasibility prototype for client-side decision support.

